title The copy step the card called the larger door, and the guard nobody could see
prompt Autonomous lap under the baton; the card's own agent-doable queue item 1, left there by this seat's last lap.
rota lap 5314, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in. Took in the concrete fact at the door before any argument: my own timed projection read step_copy_s=28.43 of 49.39, so the copy was 58 percent of a publish and the card's elder figure of 45-of-74 was a reading of a different hour rather than a different mechanism.
think Read the card whole, the claim board, and the thread. Queue item 1 asked whether the copy step wanted a design and Keaton's word -- an incremental seed sync -- or a lap. Probed before claiming rather than answering from the card: over this tree's 18,684 tracked paths a cp-per-file loop runs 71.82s where one cpio -pdum pass-through runs 1.54s, and the two trees agree on every byte and on mode, size, mtime, type and symlink target across all 18,672 non-gitlink entries. So the cost was starting cp, not moving bytes, and neither a cache nor a sync was owed.
think Claimed, committed, rebased through a claim-board conflict, pushed to both remotes, then launched the cold endurance run detached with --cadence-slice 1. The rebase offered a resolution that could have dropped a peer's claim; kept both and diffed the result against xy/main to prove my addition was the only change.
think Wrote step 7 as one cpio pass-through with the elder loop kept as the fallback, and named the tool in SOW_COPY_TOOL so a host without cpio -- gone from POSIX since 2001 -- answers rather than breaks. A pass-through that FAILS refuses out loud rather than retrying: a copy that moved nothing reads exactly like a field with nothing in it, which is the fault batch_match already guards one step over.
think My claim also priced a refusal for a directory candidate, reasoning that cp -a recurses a gitlink where the pass-through writes an empty room. Both halves were wrong about this script. Step 1 drops every directory with [ -f "$f" ] || continue, three hundred lines earlier, so the class was already empty and my refusal was a fence in a field with no gate. I had read my own probe -- git ls-files into a cp loop -- rather than the script it was standing in for.
think Took the refusal back out and put the proof where the property lives: the pen plants a gitlink, watches it reach neither the candidate list nor the seed, and a mutation drops that one filter and watches an empty room ship. A guard doing invisible work now has a mutation naming it, which is worth more than the second fence would have been.
obs The cold endurance run named two reds and one is mine by construction. sow_allow_reach_control shims cp to prove a manifest changed during copying earns no receipt, and my streaming step no longer calls cp -- so the plant reached a command the code does not run and read producer_moved/refuses got [0] want [2]. Shimmed both copy paths; 52 passing became 55. The guard caught it on the lap it landed, which is the instrument working.
obs fold_shelf_link_repoint was the second red and repaired three links in one pass -- two on a peer's 083040 shelf and one on my own new shelf, whose shelved account carried a card-relative link. Both guards GREEN after.
obs The scrub is the projection's largest step now at 19.91s in 1,430 sed processes, and it is genuinely one per file since each writes its own destination. Folding it means unscrubbed maintainer-naming bytes standing in seed/ before being rewritten in place -- clean by trust rather than by construction -- so it stays as written and the trade is named on the card for Keaton rather than taken.
obs The cold-run and build collision fired a fourth time and is now sharper than the ordering I recommended last lap. I did claim, push, launch, then build, exactly as I asked for -- and a cold run takes some twenty-five minutes, so a lap either idles for it or writes the very bytes it is reading. My two whole-field parity projections disagreed on every mtime for that reason: the run was regenerating pages beside them. A cold endurance run and a build cannot share one lap's clock.
